hese problems is because those that were in power and could have fixed this problem before now, didn't and cost, it cost lives. And so, now that I'm here, I, we just, we just, um, introduced the people's response act to make sure that we are looking at the money, that money that should be going to social safety nests, to make sure that our, our community members who are, um, living with, um, mental health, um, issues, um, are, are able to function and live in society, um, the way that, uh, that anyone would ask to. So, I don't believe, as far as my, as far as my colleagues, you know, I absolutely empathize. I empathize, but you know what? The same thing that the Republicans would do, which is figure out how to work with this on a comms basis. That's what we have to do. My job is to save lives, the lives in my community, because when we're talking about every single year, increasing the budget for police, and then, and then, and then the budget for, like, health and human services continuing to shrink in St. Louis, being number one for police violence year after year after year, number one, number two for homicides year and year after year. one? So when we're adding more money to the police, but we're still dying. So, someone has to change. Yeah, and I hear what you're saying, but I also heard you say that you think it's a comms problem. Is it that? Because... no. No, I'm saying that we can also, that that's another way that you can tackle this. You have to tackle it from more than one place. We have to work on what we want to say. What is our message? But then we also have to understand it. We have to save lives, too. St. Louis can't keep being put on the back burner at all. And I'm here to stand up for my community. Congresswoman Corey Bush, thank you so much for joining me this morning. Absolutely. Thank you.
